# Prescribed-phase source/scaffold alignment — audit report

**Run type:** theorem promotion / Target A source-to-response audit  
**Repository state at promotion:** v1.7.3 plus Sections 154–155  
**Level I status:** STILL OPEN

The canonical proof is in
[`docs/targets/target-a/08-prescribed-phase-alignment.md`](../targets/target-a/08-prescribed-phase-alignment.md).
This report records the promotion decision and scope audit.

## Promoted results

For the genuine source \(A\subset H\), every prescribed rotated/translated unit
triangular lattice phase \(Y=\Lambda'\cap H\) satisfies
\[
\nu(Y,A)\ge |Y|-\widehat p_H,\qquad
M-|Y|\le \frac76\operatorname{per}H+1,
\]
where \(M=\alpha_1(H)=N+\widehat p_H\). The proof is the exact fixed-source Hall
inequality plus inner-parallel-body Voronoi counting and Oler's inequality; no
zero-slack assumption is made.

For an independently chosen smaller optimum \(X_m\), the compatible collar
\(L=\operatorname{conv}(X_m)+t\mathbb B\) and the source hull \(H\) have a common
circumcenter placement with Hausdorff distance at most
\(3\,3^{1/4}\sqrt D\). For \(D>16\sqrt3\), if
\[
\rho\ge \frac{\pi D}{2}+\frac{4D}{c}
 \left(1+3\,3^{1/4}\sqrt D\right),\qquad c=\frac{2\sqrt3}{\pi},
\]
then \(\operatorname{conv}(X_m)+\mathbb B\subset H\), and the exact source Hall
bound applies simultaneously in the common placement to \(X_m\) and all inner
scaffold lattice sites.

## Audit and limitation

The Bonnesen input is used only for compact convex planar bodies \(H\) and \(L\), with
their exact perimeter/area deficit supplied by the canonical Oler-shell estimates.
The exact external source is Daniel A. Klain, [*An Error Estimate for the
Isoperimetric Deficit*](https://faculty.uml.edu/dklain/Klain-Isoperimetric.pdf),
*Illinois Journal of Mathematics* 49 (2005), 981–992, Section 4, equation (6):
\(P^2-4\pi A\ge\pi^2(R-r)^2\) for a compact convex planar set with perimeter
\(P\), area \(A\), circumradius \(R\), and inradius \(r\). Both bodies in Section
155 meet those hypotheses exactly.
The conclusion is a genuine coordinate-coupling theorem in the displayed
very-super-boundary subbranch. It does not identify the two matchings, preserve the
strict-rattler set, produce a Schur-kernel vector, or produce a strict smaller-\(D\)
competitor. The exact remaining lemma is the two-shadow compatibility statement in
Section 155.

The free aligned Kuperberg support estimate is not used in this promotion; in
particular, no finite-container exterior-compatibility claim is being inferred.
